This experiment develops a C program to copy the contents of one file to another using open(), read(), write(), and close() system calls.
The program is created and executed in Ubuntu using the GCC compiler.
The strace utility is used to trace the system calls generated by the cat sample.txt command.
The important system calls observed are openat(), read(), write(), and close().
During execution, control moves from user space to kernel space through system calls, where the kernel performs the requested file operations.
The copied file is checked using the cat copy.txt command to verify that the contents are copied correctly.
The strace output is saved in a file for analyzing the sequence of system calls and the kernel services involved.
The experiment helps to understand file operations and the interaction between user programs and the Linux kernel.
